<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  TWO REGIONS THAT RUN AT ONCE — the smallest thing that can answer whether
  `<parallel>` is usable in this tree.

  ⚠⚠⚠ WHY THIS IS ASKED BEFORE ANYTHING IS BUILT ON IT. `probe_parent.scxml`'s
  argument, one construct over: the generator has parallel handling and the
  conformance suite has parallel tests, and both of those are facts about
  SOMEWHERE ELSE until a document in THIS crate compiles and runs one. Nothing
  shipped here has ever used `<parallel>` — measured, zero occurrences across
  all six statecharts — so every claim about running several loops at once is a
  claim about a construct this crate has never executed.

  ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE FAILURE MODE IS ONE FIXTURES DO NOT CATCH, WHICH IS THE WHOLE
  REASON THIS EXISTS. SCE's own suite records a parallel defect that shipped in
  `1419a050ed` — a self-transition whose exit set swallowed the parallel root —
  and says it was invisible to every W3C fixture BECAUSE THEY ARE ALL ONE REGION
  DEEP. A probe that only starts two regions and stops would be one region deep
  in the way that matters, and would pass while the defect was live.

  What is being asked, precisely, and each is a separate `NO` worth having:

    1. does a document carrying `<parallel>` compile at all through this crate's
       build script;
    2. do BOTH regions actually enter — an engine that ran only the first would
       look identical from outside until the second one's work was needed;
    3. does an event reach BOTH regions, so two loops could be driven by one
       pump rather than each needing its own;
    4. ⚠⚠⚠ DOES A SELF-TRANSITION INSIDE ONE REGION LEAVE THE OTHER REGION
       ALONE — the exact shape of the defect above. The AI loop's own
       `working` state takes a self-arrow on `Null`, on every look that found
       nothing, so a supervisor running N loops in parallel takes that arrow
       constantly. If its exit set swallows the parallel root, the sibling loop
       is silently exited.

  ⚠ AND THE FIRST THING THIS PROBE MEASURED WAS ITSELF: the draft wrote that
  self-arrow the way a journal does, with a double hyphen, which XML forbids
  inside a comment. `sce-build` reported `The document has no document element` —
  the parser's answer to a comment that never closed, naming line 0 for a fault
  ten lines in. **A build-time XML error points at the file, not at the mistake**,
  which is worth knowing before a document six times this size fails the same way.

  Answering NO to any of them is as useful as YES: it is the difference between
  designing a supervisor that runs loops concurrently and filing an upstream
  ask.

  ⚠ The two regions are deliberately NOT symmetric. `ticking` takes the
  self-transition; `watching` only counts what reaches it. A probe whose regions
  did the same thing could not tell "both regions got the event" from "one
  region got it twice".
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0"
       name="probe_parallel"
       datamodel="ecmascript"
       initial="both">

  <datamodel>
    <!-- How many times the self-transition fired in its own region. -->
    <data id="ticks" expr="0"/>
    <!-- How many of the same events the SIBLING region saw. If broadcast works
         these track each other; if the sibling was exited, this stops moving. -->
    <data id="seen" expr="0"/>
    <!-- ⚠⚠⚠ How many ticks region A took while it could SEE the order standing in
         region B — question 6's answer. Zero after an order was given means
         `In()` cannot read across regions here, and standing orders have to be a
         boolean instead of a state. -->
    <data id="noticed" expr="0"/>
  </datamodel>

  <parallel id="both">

    <!--
      REGION A — takes the arrow that the defect is about. `tick` targets its own
      state, so the exit set of that transition is where a parallel root can be
      swallowed.
    -->
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ REGION A IS COMPOUND, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN TIDINESS.
      The first draft put this region's `<final>` as a SIBLING of the regions,
      directly under `<parallel>` — and `get_parallel_regions` then answered
      THREE, counting the final as a region of its own. A driver enumerating
      regions would have found one that is not one.

      A region's own ending belongs INSIDE it. That is also the arrangement the
      real design needs: the work region finishes while the orders region never
      does, which cannot even be expressed if a final is a sibling of both.
    -->
    <state id="ticking" initial="counting">

      <state id="counting">
        <!--
          ⚠⚠⚠⚠ QUESTION 6, AND IT MUST COME FIRST: can a transition in ONE region
          be guarded on what the OTHER region is doing?

          This is the shape the stand-down handle needs. A person's order arrives
          in the orders region while work is going on; the work region then has
          to ask, at its own decision point, *has that order been given* — and
          W3C SCXML's predicate for exactly that is `In('<state>')`.

          ⚠⚠ WHY NOT A `<data>` FLAG. An order the orders region sets as a
          BOOLEAN is invisible in the run's walk: nothing records when it arrived
          or what the run was doing at the time. An order held as a STATE is in
          the journal by construction, which is this workspace's rule for
          machines everywhere else — `context_review` argues it at length for a
          sub-analysis, and an order is not less of a thing than that.

          ⚠⚠⚠ SO THE FLAG IS THE FALLBACK, AND THIS IS WHAT DECIDES WHICH. The
          generator has `In` handling and the conformance suite has `In` tests,
          and both are facts about SOMEWHERE ELSE — the same sentence this file
          opens with, one predicate over. If it does not work here, standing
          orders are a boolean and the walk loses them.

          Ordered ABOVE the plain `tick` arrow deliberately: a guarded transition
          that lost its race with an unguarded sibling would look exactly like a
          guard that evaluated false.
        -->
        <transition event="tick" cond="In('alerted')" target="counting">
          <assign location="noticed" expr="noticed + 1"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="tick" target="counting">
          <assign location="ticks" expr="ticks + 1"/>
        </transition>
        <!--
          ⚠⚠⚠ QUESTION 5's DOOR: region A can finish while region B never will.
          See the note under region B for what rests on the answer.
        -->
        <transition event="finish" target="done"/>
      </state>

      <final id="done"/>
    </state>

    <!--
      REGION B — never self-transitions, so if this stops counting while region A
      keeps ticking, the sibling was exited rather than merely quiet. It is the
      control for region A, and the reason the two regions differ.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND IT NEVER FINISHES, WHICH IS QUESTION 5. A design that puts STANDING
      ORDERS in their own region beside the work has exactly this shape: the work
      region ends, and the orders region has no ending because an order is not a
      task. SCXML completes a `<parallel>` only when EVERY region is final — so
      the question a driver's whole ending detection rests on is whether a run
      whose work is over can be SEEN to be over.

      This region is deliberately the one with no final, so the probe measures
      that arrangement rather than the tidy one.
    -->
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ AND IT IS COMPOUND NOW, WHICH IS QUESTION 6's OTHER HALF. An order that
      is a STATE needs somewhere to move TO, so this region has two: the resting
      one it starts in, and the one an order puts it in. It still never reaches a
      final — an order is not a task and has no ending — so question 5 is
      unchanged by the added depth.
    -->
    <state id="watching" initial="unalerted">
      <transition event="tick">
        <assign location="seen" expr="seen + 1"/>
      </transition>

      <state id="unalerted">
        <!-- The order arriving. ⚠ It moves this region and touches nothing of
             region A's, which is the whole point: work carries on, and what
             changed is only what work can now ASK. -->
        <transition event="alert" target="alerted"/>
      </state>

      <state id="alerted"/>
    </state>

  </parallel>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠ QUESTION 5, IN REGION A: what does an engine say when ONE region is done
    and the other never will be?

    `done` is region A's own final. Reaching it must leave the sibling running —
    that is questions 2 to 4 again, one arrangement further on — and the answer a
    driver reads (`is_in_final_state`, `get_current_state`, the active set) is
    what decides whether the ORDERS-REGION design is buildable at all.

    ⚠ Answering NO is as useful as YES: it is the difference between putting
    standing orders in their own region and repeating one transition on every
    state of a flat machine, which this workspace has already paid for once as
    *two copies of one rule, and the failure of letting them drift is silent*.
  -->
</scxml>
